goal: |
  Write an optimized GPU implementation of the reference PyTorch module and make
  it as fast as possible while staying numerically correct.

  Problem: {problem_name}

  Workspace:
    problem/reference.py  — the reference `Model` (nn.Module), plus `get_inputs()`
                            and `get_init_inputs()`. Read it to see exactly what
                            to compute.
    solution.py           — YOUR CODE (edit this). Define `ModelNew`, an nn.Module
                            with the SAME constructor signature and forward output
                            as `Model`, but faster.
    check_solution.py     — static reward-hack screen (runs first).
    eval_solution.py      — checks correctness (allclose over several seeded
                            trials) and measures speedup vs the reference.

  How you optimize is open: custom CUDA C++ or Triton kernels, operator fusion,
  torch.compile, better memory layout, etc. `ModelNew(*get_init_inputs())` is
  constructed and called as `model(*get_inputs())`, same as the reference.

  Rules (correctness is measured, not trusted — the harness defends itself):
    - Only edit solution.py.
    - You may read and run the evaluation scripts to understand the public
      benchmark. Do not modify them or access/alter their runtime state.
    - Do NOT manipulate timing, RNG, CUDA streams, or precision/backend flags.
      Solutions that do are rejected.
    - Do not make network requests or download code, data, or dependencies from
      solution.py. The final kernel must be self-contained.
    - Optimize the actual computation. A real, honest speedup on a real kernel is
      the goal; a >2x speedup on a well-known op is suspicious — double-check it.
    - Run `python eval_solution.py` to test.

constraints:
  - python check_solution.py
  - command: python eval_solution.py --check-only
    timeout: {evaluation_timeout}

metrics:
  - command: python eval_solution.py
    values:
      mean_speedup: higher
    timeout: {evaluation_timeout}

acceptance:
  # eval_solution.py emits mean_speedup only on success. This gate makes a
  # missing/zero result reject the iteration instead of merging unmeasured code.
  metric_gates:
    - metric: mean_speedup
      max_regression_pct: 99

scope:
  editable: [solution.py]

agent:
  type: claude
  model: {agent_model}

budget:
  iterations: {iterations}
